WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.470
Instructor: Next up, let's talk about

00:01.470 --> 00:03.120
deleting files and folders.

00:03.120 --> 00:05.040
This is the last important thing.

00:05.040 --> 00:07.170
Well, there are other important commands,

00:07.170 --> 00:09.030
but this is the last of the fundamentals

00:09.030 --> 00:11.610
of working with files and navigating.

00:11.610 --> 00:15.450
So to delete a file, we have a command R M.

00:15.450 --> 00:18.150
I assume it stands for remove.

00:18.150 --> 00:21.120
And all we need to do is R M and then a space

00:21.120 --> 00:23.280
and then reference a file name,

00:23.280 --> 00:25.770
assuming that the file is in our current directory,

00:25.770 --> 00:27.870
or we need to reference the correct path

00:27.870 --> 00:29.520
to get to that file.

00:29.520 --> 00:31.080
Now the thing you need to know upfront is

00:31.080 --> 00:34.140
that this is not gonna put it in your trash can

00:34.140 --> 00:36.240
or your recycling bin or whatever it's called.

00:36.240 --> 00:37.680
This deletes the file.

00:37.680 --> 00:39.000
It is gone.

00:39.000 --> 00:41.160
So you wanna be careful with this, right?

00:41.160 --> 00:43.470
You don't want to go around deleting crucial files

00:43.470 --> 00:45.145
although this is a Git course

00:45.145 --> 00:48.270
and Git can help solve or alleviate these issues.

00:48.270 --> 00:50.010
If you delete something accidentally

00:50.010 --> 00:51.510
if it was being tracked by Git

00:51.510 --> 00:53.520
we can restore it with a single command.

00:53.520 --> 00:55.080
It's very easy and painless.

00:55.080 --> 00:57.360
Anyway, that's getting ahead of ourselves.

00:57.360 --> 00:58.920
Let me demonstrate how this works.

00:58.920 --> 01:02.189
Just remember, this permanently deletes the file.

01:02.189 --> 01:04.920
So what should we delete?

01:04.920 --> 01:05.880
If you wanna follow along

01:05.880 --> 01:08.820
you might want to just make something to delete first.

01:08.820 --> 01:10.803
So you could do touch, delete me.

01:12.060 --> 01:14.700
So I didn't even give it a file extension.

01:14.700 --> 01:16.650
If we look at it, where am I?

01:16.650 --> 01:18.600
I'm in the turtles directory.

01:18.600 --> 01:20.970
I could always do P W D to verify.

01:20.970 --> 01:22.560
So that's right here.

01:22.560 --> 01:24.090
Here's the file I made.

01:24.090 --> 01:25.440
I could even rename it from here.

01:25.440 --> 01:30.440
How about we make it a .D doc X file

01:30.840 --> 01:33.360
which is Microsoft Word, and it's gonna ask me

01:33.360 --> 01:35.580
are you sure you wanna do that?

01:35.580 --> 01:36.413
It doesn't matter.

01:36.413 --> 01:37.260
I'm gonna delete it.

01:37.260 --> 01:39.960
But I wanted to show you, if you make modifications here

01:39.960 --> 01:41.130
I could even rename this,

01:41.130 --> 01:43.000
delete me with a bunch of M or

01:44.352 --> 01:45.185
with a bunch of E's at the end.

01:45.185 --> 01:48.630
If I type L S now, this is up to date as well.

01:48.630 --> 01:50.940
Okay? So to delete it, it's just R M

01:50.940 --> 01:52.590
and then the name of the file.

01:52.590 --> 01:54.690
And that's because I'm in this folder,

01:54.690 --> 01:57.090
so I don't need to reference some other path.

01:57.090 --> 01:58.890
This is exactly what I need.

01:58.890 --> 02:01.263
If I hit enter, you can see it disappears.

02:02.160 --> 02:03.330
Whoops, that's not L S.

02:03.330 --> 02:04.590
I meant to type L S.

02:04.590 --> 02:05.460
It's gone.

02:05.460 --> 02:06.810
So it's not coming back.

02:06.810 --> 02:08.850
We're not using Git right now.

02:08.850 --> 02:12.120
So that is gone, that that file is totally gone.

02:12.120 --> 02:15.570
So let's make two more files to delete just really quickly.

02:15.570 --> 02:19.237
How about delete.txt and remove.xls?

02:20.910 --> 02:22.563
That's, I think that's Excel?

02:23.700 --> 02:24.840
Maybe not.

02:24.840 --> 02:26.280
Well, whatever that extension is.

02:26.280 --> 02:27.113
I thought it was Excel.

02:27.113 --> 02:28.020
It doesn't matter.

02:28.020 --> 02:29.430
I'm gonna delete them.

02:29.430 --> 02:30.990
So I'll do 'em together.

02:30.990 --> 02:31.980
They're both here.

02:31.980 --> 02:33.870
You can see them there.

02:33.870 --> 02:38.870
R M, delete.txt space, remove.xls.

02:39.138 --> 02:41.100
They're both gone.

02:41.100 --> 02:43.680
Okay, quickly, I'll show it over here.

02:43.680 --> 02:48.120
Let's delete this cyan file that I made on my Windows side.

02:48.120 --> 02:51.510
So here it is, cyan.js,

02:51.510 --> 02:55.143
R M cyan.js.

02:57.062 --> 02:58.050
If I type L S, it does not show up here.

02:58.050 --> 02:59.340
And if I look over here

02:59.340 --> 03:02.070
in the the file explorer, it's not here.

03:02.070 --> 03:04.800
I don't, maybe there's a way to make this larger.

03:04.800 --> 03:06.208
There we go.

03:06.208 --> 03:07.350
Well still it's not here.

03:07.350 --> 03:09.600
Okay, so the next thing I wanna talk

03:09.600 --> 03:11.490
about is deleting folders.

03:11.490 --> 03:13.200
If I wanna delete this folder,

03:13.200 --> 03:17.490
land turtles right there.

03:17.490 --> 03:20.490
I cannot use the R M command as it stands.

03:20.490 --> 03:22.320
Land turtles like that.

03:22.320 --> 03:25.230
It says nope, land turtles is a directory.

03:25.230 --> 03:29.850
R M deletes files or at least R M the way we used it.

03:29.850 --> 03:34.050
So we actually have a variation of R M in order

03:34.050 --> 03:37.020
to delete a folder or a directory.

03:37.020 --> 03:39.900
R M dash R F.

03:39.900 --> 03:42.030
We haven't talked about flags.

03:42.030 --> 03:42.863
This is, you know

03:42.863 --> 03:45.870
just a crash course on the basic terminal navigation,

03:45.870 --> 03:47.760
the essential commands you need to know.

03:47.760 --> 03:49.800
But all of the commands we've used actually

03:49.800 --> 03:53.520
accept a bunch of options that modify how they behave.

03:53.520 --> 03:55.020
So as a quick example

03:55.020 --> 03:58.860
L S actually has a couple of other variations.

03:58.860 --> 04:03.300
One is L S dash A, which is gonna show me hidden files

04:03.300 --> 04:05.850
so that dash A modifies the behavior of L S.

04:05.850 --> 04:08.880
It says list all files including hidden,

04:08.880 --> 04:10.200
hidden files start with a dot.

04:10.200 --> 04:11.450
So there's a hidden file.

04:12.330 --> 04:15.270
This will actually come up when we make a Git repository.

04:15.270 --> 04:17.100
It makes a hidden directory for us.

04:17.100 --> 04:19.290
Anyway, getting ahead of myself.

04:19.290 --> 04:22.050
So these flags are different options we can pass in.

04:22.050 --> 04:25.080
And there are two we will use on R M

04:25.080 --> 04:26.850
if we want to delete a directory,

04:26.850 --> 04:28.650
R and F.

04:28.650 --> 04:30.600
We don't need to go too much into what they mean.

04:30.600 --> 04:31.860
They stand for recursive,

04:31.860 --> 04:34.290
that's the R and force is the F.

04:34.290 --> 04:36.390
Basically, this is how we delete a folder.

04:38.396 --> 04:39.248
But you wanna be careful with this

04:39.248 --> 04:40.920
because just like with deleting a file

04:40.920 --> 04:43.259
from the command line or from the terminal, it's gone.

04:43.259 --> 04:45.720
So if you delete a directory that's full

04:45.720 --> 04:48.150
of important stuff and it's not backed up somewhere,

04:48.150 --> 04:50.880
it's not in the cloud, it's gone.

04:50.880 --> 04:52.563
That's kind of end of story.

04:53.812 --> 04:57.510
So let's say I wanna delete the entire turtles directory.

04:57.510 --> 05:00.090
Why don't I start by deleting land turtles?

05:00.090 --> 05:01.890
I didn't mean to type this by the way,

05:01.890 --> 05:03.690
so I wanna delete land turtles.

05:03.690 --> 05:06.270
Again, R M land turtles does not work

05:06.270 --> 05:11.270
but R M dash R F land turtles will, it's gone.

05:11.820 --> 05:13.323
There's no land turtles here,

05:14.520 --> 05:16.260
but that was an empty directory.

05:16.260 --> 05:18.000
If I try and delete turtles,

05:18.000 --> 05:20.730
which itself has, it doesn't have a lot,

05:20.730 --> 05:23.850
but turtles has sea turtles folder,

05:23.850 --> 05:26.460
and George.Py, a file.

05:26.460 --> 05:28.350
I'm now in the directory above it.

05:28.350 --> 05:31.482
I'm in pets, so I'm gonna try deleting it.

05:31.482 --> 05:35.370
So I'll clear, by the way, on a Mac that shortcut

05:35.370 --> 05:38.970
if I have a bunch of stuff to clear is command K.

05:38.970 --> 05:40.750
Anyway, I'm in the pets directory

05:42.206 --> 05:45.363
and if I wanna delete turtles, R M dash R F turtles.

05:46.752 --> 05:48.102
So you'll see it over here.

05:49.129 --> 05:51.270
There it is, and there it goes.

05:51.270 --> 05:52.103
It's gone.

05:52.103 --> 05:52.980
And that's it.

05:52.980 --> 05:56.250
I can't navigate into turtles, it doesn't exist.

05:56.250 --> 05:58.350
No such file or directory.

05:58.350 --> 06:00.870
But remember I need the dash R F to do that

06:00.870 --> 06:02.280
if it's a folder.

06:02.280 --> 06:04.560
If it's just a file, I just do R M.

06:04.560 --> 06:07.260
And either way those are gone.

06:07.260 --> 06:08.490
They're gone for good.

06:08.490 --> 06:09.960
So be careful, you know

06:09.960 --> 06:11.940
don't go around running this command

06:11.940 --> 06:14.700
on very important files and folders.

06:14.700 --> 06:16.918
Just always take a moment to think about what you're doing

06:16.918 --> 06:19.050
because you don't have that intermediate step

06:19.050 --> 06:21.240
of dragging something to the trash.

06:21.240 --> 06:23.160
But not much of this course really relies

06:23.160 --> 06:24.600
on deleting stuff, to be honest.

06:24.600 --> 06:26.820
It's mostly creating things

06:26.820 --> 06:29.970
and tracking those things with Git and changing those files.

06:29.970 --> 06:31.020
So it's not like we're going

06:31.020 --> 06:32.430
around deleting a bunch of stuff.

06:32.430 --> 06:34.200
I just figured you should know about those commands

06:34.200 --> 06:36.180
'cause they will come up every so often.

06:36.180 --> 06:38.730
All right, so that's R M and R M dash R F.

06:38.730 --> 06:40.080
They're both the same command.

06:40.080 --> 06:42.633
Just a variation to delete folders.
